<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Godin Tepe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Godin Tepe is an archaeological site in the Kangavar valley of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Excavations found a walled enclave with Uruk-style pottery and tablets of the late fourth millennium BCE, early chemical evidence of wine and beer, and a fortified manor of the Median period of the eighth to sixth centuries BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Godin Tepe lies in the Luristan region, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> province, on the left bank of the Gamas Āb river, and its importance may have come from its role as a trading outpost in the early Mesopotamian networks. It was found in a survey in 1961 and excavated from 1965 to 1973 by a Canadian expedition under T. Cuyler Young Jr., sponsored by the Royal Ontario Museum. Because its layers lie so deep, the nearby site of Seh Gabi, 6 km to the northeast, was dug to study the earliest periods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was inhabited by about 5200 BCE. In Level VIII, about 4200–4000 BCE, it lay within growing trade networks in metals and stones such as lapis lazuli from Badakshan. In the late fourth millennium the pottery of Level VI.I shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> influence, with parallels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, Uruk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, and merchants from Susa may have kept a trading post there. Thirteen seal impressions, two cylinder seals and 38 numerical tablets, mostly accounts, were found. A large Oval Enclosure was burnt about 3000 BCE, its debris full of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> bullets, though one researcher has argued that the enclosure did not exist.</p><p>Level IV, about 3000–2650 BCE, probably marks the arrival of people of the Kura–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> culture from the north. Level III, from about 2600 BCE, has links with Susa and much of Luristan and may have belonged to the Elamite confederacy. Near 1400 BCE the site was abandoned, and it stayed empty until about 750 BCE.</p><p>The new settlers built a fortification wall three metres thick with arrow slits and a hall of 30 wooden columns, 24 by 28 metres inside, which the excavators took for the palace or manor house of a powerful local figure. Its pottery matches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> sites such as Bābā Jān Tepe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nush-i-jan">Tepe Nush-i Jan</a> and Pasargadae. Godin was abandoned again in the 6th century BCE, perhaps in the face of the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, and its last building was an Islamic shrine of about the 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mound covers about 15 hectares and rises 30 metres above the plain, its northern end eroded to a cliff. It is divided into a low Outer Town to the south, an Upper Citadel and a Citadel, and has been damaged by gullies, by digging for brick earth and farm soil, by an Islamic cemetery and by a modern road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Godin Tepe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Deioces' historicity is debated, but Assyrian records and excavated sites such as <b>Godin Tepe</b> and Nush-i Jan attest to growing Median power in this period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The city depended on materials it lacked, such as timber, stone and metals, and in the so-called Uruk expansion settlers founded outposts and colonies far up the Euphrates in Syria and south-eastern Anatolia, such as Habuba Kabira and Jebel Aruda, and in western Iran at <b>Godin Tepe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
